# {{ app_display_name }} (iOS)

A small SwiftUI app that uses this project's `/api/v1` API: sign in (or create a local account), see your session,
list, create, edit and delete items, sign out. It is an architecture to extend, not a design system.

| | |
|---|---|
| Minimum iOS | **17.0** (`@Observable`, `NavigationStack`, `ContentUnavailableView`; see `Config/Shared.xcconfig`) |
| Language | Swift 6 (strict concurrency), SwiftUI, async/await |
| API client | generated by Apple's Swift OpenAPI Generator from `Contract/openapi.json`, URLSession transport |
| Token storage | Keychain, `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly`, never synchronised |
| Signing | none needed for the simulator |

## Open it

Open `ios/App.xcodeproj` in Xcode (26 or newer), choose an iPhone simulator, and run. The Debug build talks to
`http://localhost:{{ debug_api_port }}`, so start the web part first (`npm run db:init && npm run db:up && npm run db:migrate`,
then `AUTH_ALLOW_SIGN_UP=true npm run dev` in the project root) and use "Create an account".
The Release build requires https to `{{ release_api_host }}`: set the real host in `Config/Release.xcconfig`.

From the command line: `sh ios/scripts/test.sh`.

## How the pieces fit

```
SwiftUI views (App/)
   │ observe
AppModel / ItemsModel (AppCore)           AuthState: launching, signedOut, signedIn, unconfirmed
   │
AuthService ── POST /api/auth/sign-in|sign-up|sign-out ──▶ Better Auth (token in `set-auth-token`)
   │ stores token
KeychainTokenStore
   │ read by
BearerAuthMiddleware ──▶ generated Client (Contract/openapi.json) ──▶ GET/POST/PATCH/DELETE /api/v1/...
```

Authentication endpoints are Better Auth's own and are called by `AuthService`, outside the contract: see the web
project's `docs/native-client.md`. Everything under `/api/v1` is typed by the generated client.

## Changing the API

1. Change the web part; run `npm run openapi:generate` there.
2. `sh ios/scripts/sync-contract.sh` (copies the contract and regenerates the client).
3. Fix what the compiler and `sh ios/scripts/test.sh` show; commit `Contract/` and the generated client together.

CI (and `sh ios/scripts/check-generated.sh`) fail when the committed client is not what the contract generates.

## The real end-to-end scenario

`node ios/scripts/e2e.mjs` starts the web app (production build) on a free port with two throwaway accounts and runs
the app's `EndToEndTests` on the simulator: sign in, token in the Keychain, session, create/read/update, a second
account is refused, then it **restarts the web server and the database**, relaunches (still signed in), deletes, signs
out, and checks the old token now gets `401`. It prints no secret.

## Device signing

Choose your team in Xcode (Signing & Capabilities) on your machine; nothing about it is committed. Set your own
bundle identifier in `Config/Shared.xcconfig` first.

## Upgrading

This project has two template layers, each upgraded on its own: the web part (`.copier-answers.yml`) and this iOS layer
(`.copier-answers.ios.yml`). With the platform CLI, from the `developer-platform` checkout:

```sh
devkit status PROJECT                                 # both layers, their releases and what is newer
devkit upgrade PROJECT --layer native --dry-run       # preview
devkit upgrade PROJECT --layer native                 # review, test and commit it before the next upgrade
devkit upgrade PROJECT --layer web --dry-run
```

`devkit upgrade` refuses a project with both layers unless `--layer` names one, and refuses a step that would leave
the two layers on releases that do not belong together (it says which layer to upgrade first). Each upgrade needs a clean git working tree (nothing modified, nothing untracked); build
products and logs of the scripts here stay in ignored directories. Without the CLI, from the project root:
`copier update --answers-file .copier-answers.ios.yml --vcs-ref vX.Y.Z --defaults --skip-answered` (the answers file
is required: without it Copier would update the web part).
